int* p = nullptr; // explicitly null
if (p) {
*p = 5; // skipped — p is null
}
Always initialise pointers. Either give them a real address or nullptr.
Where I got stuck: my first month of C++ I declared int* p; and immediately did *p = 5;. Garbage address → memory access violation → crash. Always either initialise from a real variable's &, set to nullptr, or use new.

int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
arr[0]; // 10
arr[4]; // 50
arr[5]; // OUT OF BOUNDS — undefined behaviour!
// size
constexpr int N = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]); // 5
C-style arrays decay to pointers when passed to functions:
void print(int* a, int n) { // received as pointer; size info LOST
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) std::cout << a[i] << " ";
}
print(arr, 5);
This is the classic C foot-gun. The array's size is lost; you must pass it separately. Forget once → buffer overrun → security CVE.

#include <array>
#include <vector>
std::array<int, 5> fixed = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}; // size at compile time
fixed.size(); // 5 — known!
std::vector<int> dynamic = {1, 2, 3}; // size at runtime
dynamic.push_back(4);
dynamic.size(); // 4
Both know their size, support range-for, and refuse to decay. Use these in 100% of new code. Save C arrays for embedded / no-std contexts.

int* p = new int(5);
delete p;
delete p; // DOUBLE FREE — undefined behaviour, often crash
int* q = new int(7);
// ... forget to delete q ... // MEMORY LEAK — process holds memory forever
int* r;
{
int local = 5;
r = &local;
}
*r; // DANGLING — local is gone
These three bugs — double-free, leak, dangling — define why manual memory management is the hardest part of C++.
Trick: in modern C++ you rarely write new and delete directly. You use smart pointers (std::unique_ptr, std::shared_ptr — Post 05) and let the type system free things automatically. This chapter is here so you understand what the smart pointers are doing for you.

int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int* p = a; // a decays to pointer to first element
*p; // 10
*(p + 1); // 20
p[2]; // 30 — same as *(p + 2)
++p; // now points to second element
// equivalent loops
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) std::cout << a[i] << " ";
for (int* q = a; q < a + 5; ++q) std::cout << *q << " ";
C-strings are char* to a null-terminated character array:
const char* s = "hello";
// s[0]='h', s[1]='e', ..., s[5]='\0'
In modern code, use std::string and std::string_view (C++17). C-strings are kept for compatibility with C APIs.

void bad() {
FILE* f = fopen("data.txt", "r");
if (!do_stuff(f)) {
return; // LEAK — never closed
}
fclose(f);
}
void good() {
std::ifstream f("data.txt"); // file opened
if (!do_stuff(f)) {
return; // SAFE — ifstream destructor closes it
}
} // closed here too if we reach the end
std::ifstream is an RAII wrapper around a file handle. The destructor closes the file no matter how the function returns (normal, early return, exception thrown).

class Resource {
int* data;
public:
Resource(int n) : data(new int[n]{}) {} // acquire
~Resource() { delete[] data; } // release
};
void use() {
Resource r(100);
// ...
} // r.~Resource() called — data freed
Trick: every C++ class that owns a raw resource (memory, file, lock, socket) should follow the Rule of Three / Five / Zero:
If you write a custom destructor, you probably also need copy constructor + copy assignment.
C++11 adds move constructor + move assignment (Five total).
If you can avoid all five by using smart pointers and containers (the Rule of Zero) — do.

Modern C++ provides smart pointers that own heap memory and free it automatically. We cover them fully in Post 05; here's a preview:
#include <memory>
// unique ownership — auto-freed when the unique_ptr goes out of scope
auto p = std::make_unique<int>(42);
std::cout << *p << "\n"; // 42
// no delete needed
// shared ownership — last owner frees
auto sp1 = std::make_shared<int>(100);
auto sp2 = sp1; // both own; reference count = 2
// when both go out of scope, memory freed
Rule: never write new or delete in new code unless you have a specific reason. Use make_unique / make_shared.

Write void swap_int(int* a, int* b) that swaps the values via pointers.
Solution
#include <iostream>
void swap_int(int* a, int* b) {
int tmp = *a;
*a = *b;
*b = tmp;
}
int main() {
int x = 1, y = 2;
swap_int(&x, &y);
std::cout << x << " " << y << "\n"; // 2 1
}

Write int sum(const int* arr, int n) that returns the sum.
Solution
int sum(const int* arr, int n) {
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) total += arr[i];
return total;
}

Rewrite Exercise 2 to take const std::vector<int>& instead of pointer + size.
Solution
#include <vector>
int sum(const std::vector<int>& v) {
int total = 0;
for (int x : v) total += x;
return total;
}

The code below leaks. Identify where, and fix it (a) with explicit delete, then (b) with std::unique_ptr.
void process() {
int* buf = new int[1024];
if (something_failed()) {
return; // LEAK
}
use(buf);
delete[] buf;
}
Solution
(a) Add delete[] buf; before the early return. Annoying — easy to miss.
(b) Use std::unique_ptr:
#include <memory>
void process() {
auto buf = std::make_unique<int[]>(1024);
if (something_failed()) {
return; // buf destructor frees automatically
}
use(buf.get());
}

